  <!-- ===== OUR APPROACH ===== -->
  <section class="prog-section" id="approach">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="eyebrow">The SHARP Method</p>
      <h2 class="prog-section__heading">How the SHARP Method works in <span class="nb">Pre-IB</span> Mathematics</h2>
      <p class="prog-section__sub">Jeremy Lim, a former corporate lawyer (LLB Hons, NUS Faculty of Law), built the SHARP Method from the way lawyers take a problem apart before answering it. Our solution manuals follow it too: every step carries a short &lsquo;Why:&rsquo;, and a &lsquo;Watch:&rsquo; flags the common slips.</p>

      <div class="card-grid card-grid--2">
        <div class="prog-card">
          <h3 class="prog-card__title"><span class="step-dot" aria-hidden="true">S</span>See</h3>
          <p class="prog-card__desc">Many students lose marks before they write a line, because they misread the question. At See, your child reads each question the way a lawyer reads a contract. They look for the command word (solve, simplify, factorise, differentiate, show that), whether it wants first principles or the rules, and which topic is being tested. With surds, See means spotting the conjugate that will rationalise the denominator. By the time your child picks up the pen, they know which method to use.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="prog-card">
          <h3 class="prog-card__title"><span class="step-dot" aria-hidden="true">H</span>Hit</h3>
          <p class="prog-card__desc">Once the question type is clear, your child picks the right tool and writes the rule down before using it. Each topic has its own procedure: the limit definition for first principles, the power rule for standard differentiation, the laws of logarithms for log equations and the conjugate method for surds. For integration, it&rsquo;s &lsquo;reverse the power rule, never drop the + C&rsquo;. Writing the rule first, as our manuals do, turns a guess into a method an examiner can give marks for.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="prog-card">
          <h3 class="prog-card__title"><span class="step-dot" aria-hidden="true">A</span>Apply</h3>
          <p class="prog-card__desc">Now the method goes onto the page, line by line, with nothing skipped. In first principles, that means substituting x + h everywhere, expanding, and cancelling the h in the denominator <em>before</em> letting h tend to zero: the move the whole method hinges on. In integration, it means carrying the + C every time. Jeremy watches each student&rsquo;s shared working live and flags missing steps and slips as they happen.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="prog-card">
          <h3 class="prog-card__title"><span class="step-dot" aria-hidden="true">R</span><span class="step-dot" aria-hidden="true">P</span>Refine &amp; Practise</h3>
          <p class="prog-card__desc">Your child checks every solution against a short checklist from our &lsquo;Watch:&rsquo; callouts: let h tend to zero rather than setting h = 0 too early, keep the + C, and give every log a positive argument. Then your child reworks key problems from memory, spaced over weeks rather than crammed before the exam, until the procedure is automatic. Our 120-question calculus drill set is built for exactly that.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ===== QUESTION-TYPE FRAMEWORKS ===== -->
  <section class="prog-section" id="frameworks">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="eyebrow">Question-type frameworks</p>
      <h2 class="prog-section__heading">A named method for every question type in <span class="nb">Pre-IB</span> Mathematics</h2>
      <p class="prog-section__sub">Your child learns which tool to reach for, and how to use it, for every topic from indices to integration.</p>

      <div class="card-grid card-grid--2">
        <div class="prog-card">
          <h3 class="prog-card__title">Differentiation from first principles</h3>
          <p class="prog-card__desc">The method never changes, so we drill it as a fixed routine. State the limit definition: f&prime;(x) = lim<sub>h&rarr;0</sub> [f(x + h) &minus; f(x)] / h. Substitute x + h for every x in the function. Expand and simplify the numerator. <strong>Cancel the h in the denominator</strong> (the move the whole method hinges on), and only <em>then</em> let h tend to zero. The classic error is setting h = 0 too early, which gives 0/0 and no marks. Our drill set works through it on every question until it&rsquo;s habit.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="prog-card">
          <h3 class="prog-card__title">Differentiation by rules</h3>
          <p class="prog-card__desc">Once first principles makes sense, speed comes from the rules, applied one term at a time. State the rule first: d/dx(x<sup>n</sup>) = n&middot;x<sup>n&minus;1</sup>. Differentiate term by term, bringing the power down and reducing it by one. Keep constants and coefficients visible, and rewrite roots and reciprocals as powers first so the rule applies directly. Your child writes the rule at the top of the working, which earns method marks and stops silent slips under time pressure.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="prog-card">
          <h3 class="prog-card__title">Tangents &amp; stationary points</h3>
          <p class="prog-card__desc">For a tangent: find dy/dx, evaluate it at the given point for the gradient, then use y &minus; y&#8321; = m(x &minus; x&#8321;). For stationary points: set dy/dx = 0, solve for x, and substitute back for y. Then classify the point as a maximum, minimum or point of inflexion, using the second-derivative test or a sign check either side. Each step is written with its reason, so the method marks stay even if the final arithmetic slips.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="prog-card">
          <h3 class="prog-card__title">Integration &amp; areas</h3>
          <p class="prog-card__desc">Integration is differentiation in reverse, with two habits to keep every time. Reverse the power rule: add one to the power and divide by the new power. For an indefinite integral, <strong>never drop the + C</strong> (the constant of integration); if a point on the curve is given, use it to find C. For a definite integral, substitute the upper limit, then the lower limit, and subtract, in that order. For an area under a curve, integrate between the limits. A dropped + C and swapped limits are the slips our &lsquo;Watch:&rsquo; callouts flag most often, so we drill both.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <details class="prog-more">
        <summary class="btn btn--sm prog-more__toggle"><span class="prog-more__show">Show all 6 frameworks</span><span class="prog-more__hide">Hide the other 2 frameworks</span></summary>
        <div class="card-grid card-grid--2">

        <div class="prog-card">
          <h3 class="prog-card__title">Indices &amp; surds</h3>
          <p class="prog-card__desc">Indices and surds sit under everything that follows, so we make the laws automatic. For indices: name the law that applies (product, quotient, power of a power, negative or fractional) and use one law per line, so each step can be checked. For surds: simplify by taking out square factors, and rationalise a denominator such as a + &radic;b by multiplying top and bottom by its conjugate, a &minus; &radic;b. The reason sits beside every line, so your child sees <em>why</em> each move comes next.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="prog-card">
          <h3 class="prog-card__title">Logarithms &amp; exponentials</h3>
          <p class="prog-card__desc">Logarithms feel hard when students treat them as a new kind of arithmetic rather than the inverse of exponentials. We tie them to one idea: a logarithm is an exponent. Convert between exponential and logarithmic form, then apply the laws (product, quotient, power and change of base) one at a time. Solve equations by isolating the logarithm or the exponential first, and always check the domain, because a logarithm only takes a positive argument.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      </details>
    </div>
  </section>

