  <!-- ===== BEFORE / AFTER ===== -->
  <section class="prog-section ba-section" id="before-after">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="eyebrow">Before &amp; after</p>
      <h2 class="prog-section__heading">The same question, before and after the SHARP Method</h2>
      <p class="prog-section__sub">What changes when a student follows the method instead of guessing. An illustrative first-principles question, not a real student script.</p>

      <div class="ba-question">
        <span class="ba-q-label">Calculus &middot; Differentiation from first principles &middot; 3 marks</span>
        <p>Differentiate f(x) = x&sup2; from first principles.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="ba-grid">
        <div class="ba-card ba-card--before">
          <span class="ba-tag ba-tag--before">Before</span>
          <p class="ba-answer">Writes &ldquo;f&prime;(x) = 2x&rdquo; with no working, or sets h = 0 straight away, reaches 0/0 and stops.</p>
          <ul class="ba-notes">
            <li>Skips the limit definition entirely</li>
            <li>Sets h = 0 too early &rarr; 0/0, which is undefined</li>
            <li>No working shown, so the method marks are lost</li>
          </ul>
          <span class="ba-mark ba-mark--before">Typically 0&ndash;1 / 3</span>
        </div>

        <div class="ba-card ba-card--after">
          <span class="ba-tag ba-tag--after">After SHARP</span>
          <p class="ba-answer">States the definition; <span class="nb">f(x + h)</span> = <span class="nb">(x + h)&sup2;</span> = <span class="nb">x&sup2; + 2xh + h&sup2;</span>; so <span class="nb">[f(x + h) &minus; f(x)] / h</span> = <span class="nb">(2xh + h&sup2;) / h</span> = <span class="nb">2x + h</span>; then lets <span class="nb">h &rarr; 0</span> &rarr; <strong class="nb">f&prime;(x) = 2x</strong>.</p>
          <ul class="ba-notes">
            <li>States the limit definition first</li>
            <li><strong>Cancels the h</strong> before letting h &rarr; 0: the move the method hinges on</li>
            <li>Every line shown, so full method marks</li>
          </ul>
          <span class="ba-mark ba-mark--after">3 / 3</span>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p class="ba-footnote">Cancelling the h before taking the limit, rather than setting h = 0 too early, is the habit we drill at the <strong>Apply</strong> step of the SHARP Method.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ===== INSIDE A LESSON ===== -->
  <section class="prog-section lesson-flow" id="lesson-flow">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="eyebrow">Inside a lesson</p>
      <h2 class="prog-section__heading">What 90 minutes at <span class="nb">A-Worthy</span> looks like</h2>
      <p class="prog-section__intro">Most parents never see inside a maths tuition lesson. Here&rsquo;s how a typical Year 3 or Year 4 lesson runs, from warm-up to homework.</p>

      <ol class="lesson-timeline">
        <li class="lesson-step">
          <span class="lesson-step__num" aria-hidden="true">01</span>
          <div class="lesson-step__body">
            <span class="lesson-step__time">0 &ndash; 5 min &middot; Retrieval warm-up</span>
            <h3 class="lesson-step__title">Five quick questions on last week</h3>
            <p class="lesson-step__desc">Each tests one of last week&rsquo;s techniques, such as a surd to rationalise, a law of logarithms or a derivative by the rules. Students answer in the chat or on their whiteboards, and anyone who&rsquo;s stuck gets a one-line reminder.</p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="lesson-step">
          <span class="lesson-step__num" aria-hidden="true">02</span>
          <div class="lesson-step__body">
            <span class="lesson-step__time">5 &ndash; 15 min &middot; Question of the week</span>
            <h3 class="lesson-step__title">Read the question before you solve</h3>
            <p class="lesson-step__desc">We put a practice question on screen and work through the <em>See</em> step together: the command word, whether it wants first principles or the rules, and the method to use. By minute fifteen, every student knows the method before writing a line.</p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="lesson-step">
          <span class="lesson-step__num" aria-hidden="true">03</span>
          <div class="lesson-step__body">
            <span class="lesson-step__time">15 &ndash; 30 min &middot; Method walkthrough</span>
            <h3 class="lesson-step__title">State the rule, then use the rule</h3>
            <p class="lesson-step__desc">First principles, the power rule, the laws of logarithms or the conjugate method for surds, whichever fits the day&rsquo;s topic. Jeremy works a model solution with a &lsquo;Why:&rsquo; on every line, then writes a flawed version on purpose for students to catch. Because the rule has a name, students can reach for it on exam day without a tutor in the room.</p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="lesson-step">
          <span class="lesson-step__num" aria-hidden="true">04</span>
          <div class="lesson-step__body">
            <span class="lesson-step__time">30 &ndash; 60 min &middot; Live problem-solving &amp; feedback</span>
            <h3 class="lesson-step__title">Three rounds of feedback per student</h3>
            <p class="lesson-step__desc">Students attempt the question on shared whiteboards while Jeremy moves between them. He flags a dropped + C, an h set to zero too early, a sign error or a missing domain check on a logarithm. With at most 6 in the group, every student gets feedback at least three times in these thirty minutes.</p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="lesson-step">
          <span class="lesson-step__num" aria-hidden="true">05</span>
          <div class="lesson-step__body">
            <span class="lesson-step__time">60 &ndash; 75 min &middot; Peer checking</span>
            <h3 class="lesson-step__title">Train the marker&rsquo;s eye</h3>
            <p class="lesson-step__desc">Students swap solutions and check each other&rsquo;s working line by line against the marking standard. Once they see what loses marks in someone else&rsquo;s working, they stop doing it in their own.</p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="lesson-step">
          <span class="lesson-step__num" aria-hidden="true">06</span>
          <div class="lesson-step__body">
            <span class="lesson-step__time">75 &ndash; 90 min &middot; Exit ticket</span>
            <h3 class="lesson-step__title">No busywork goes home</h3>
            <p class="lesson-step__desc">A two-question exit ticket checks what stuck. Jeremy sets one focused practice piece, usually fifteen minutes&rsquo; work, and sends an annotated worked solution on WhatsApp the next morning. Each piece targets a weakness spotted in that lesson.</p>
          </div>
        </li>
      </ol>

      <p class="lesson-flow__note">Lessons run weekly on the A-Worthy Whiteboard, and every one is recorded. If your child misses a week, the recording, worksheet and homework reach them within 24 hours.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

